#!/usr/bin/env sh
# Pre-commit hook for the assurance playbook itself.
#
# SCOPE, deliberately narrow. This repo does NOT self-apply the playbook — it has
# no remote, no CI and one maintainer, and full self-application would be
# ceremony. Three things earn their place, and each was earned rather than
# assumed:
#
# (This sentence said "Two things ... and both" while three were listed below
# it, for as long as the third has existed — in the hook whose own item 2 exists
# because stated counts go stale. Fixed on sight; it is the sixth count error
# recorded on this branch.)
#
#   1. The skill-copy freshness check. The two copies drifted for a full
#      release, leaving guidance here that the copy which actually runs lacked.
#
#   2. The documentation-consistency check. Eight of the nine regressions on
#      this branch were in markdown — a moved directory, a renumbered phase, a
#      stale count — and none had any mechanical protection; every one was found
#      by running the same greps again by hand. It checks claims about the
#      present that resolve to a name, and nothing about wording.
#
#   3. The gate regression suites. `quality-gates.md` invariant 14 says a gate
#      that can be silently narrowed owes a regression test, and the two gates
#      this repo ships as executable code were broken by their own fixes four
#      times — once by a change that would have blocked the first commit of
#      every bootstrapped repo. Every case in those suites is one a review
#      found, not one their author thought of. Running them here is the only
#      thing standing between a plausible edit and a fifth round of that.
#
#      `check-docs.test.sh` joined them after the doc gate — the newest of the
#      three, and the only one that had shipped with no suite at all — lost six
#      ways on its first adversarial pass, four of them silently, printing its
#      success line with its counts unchanged while the defect walked through.
#      Invariant 14 is not satisfied by a gate being careful; it is satisfied by
#      a suite that fails when the gate stops working.
#
# Adding anything else is a decision to make on purpose, not by accretion.
#
# ACTIVATION: `git config core.hooksPath .githooks`. Committing this file does not
# arm it — a hook in the tree that nobody configured is exactly the kind of
# decoration this playbook exists to prevent. Verify with:
#   git config core.hooksPath   # expect: .githooks
#
# It is repo-WIDE, not additive: setting it makes git ignore `.git/hooks/*`
# entirely. Harmless here (that directory holds only samples), but any hook
# installed there later stops running silently, so a second hook belongs in this
# directory rather than that one.
#
# It runs on every commit rather than only on commits touching the skill, because
# the drift this catches is usually introduced by editing the INSTALLED copy —
# which leaves no trace in this repo's staged changes at all.

set -e

repo_root="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"

sh "$repo_root/runners/claude/check-skill-sync.sh"
sh "$repo_root/runners/claude/check-skill-sync.test.sh"
sh "$repo_root/reference/node-pnpm/test/gates.test.sh"
sh "$repo_root/scripts/check-docs.test.sh"
node "$repo_root/scripts/check-docs.mjs"
